meta { title = "Arrays and mapping", description = "Create arrays, transform their elements and control local write access.", order = 6, group = "Language reference" }

An array type is written [T]. Array lengths may vary at runtime; its elements keep one type. Arrays and tensors are different values, with explicit conversions between supported numeric forms.

h2 { "Create and read an array" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> int:
  values: [int] = [10, 20, 30]
  values[1] + length(values)
""" }

Indexes are zero-based and bounds-checked. Empty arrays need an element type from an annotation or surrounding context. Arrays can contain the supported numeric, Bool, String, record, enum and function-value types; this does not imply arbitrary nested arrays or arrays of tensors.

h2 { "Map sequentially" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn double(value: int) -> int:
  value * 2

fn main() -> [int]:
  [1, 2, 3] -> map(double)
""" }

map evaluates its callback sequentially and preserves input order. A supported callback can capture immutable values or return another supported element type. For CPU task scheduling, choose parallelMap explicitly.

h2 { "Construct by index" }

Unreleased language branch: generate constructs a fresh array by calling a function with each index. It does not require an intermediate array of indices.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> [int]:
  offset = 10
  generate(4, fn(index): index * 2 + offset)
""" }

The result is [10, 12, 14, 16]. Indices run from zero up to count, excluding count, in ascending order. A zero count calls no callbacks. The count is a nonnegative int; invalid runtime counts and excessive allocation sizes produce checked faults. Callbacks follow the same completed-element and capture rules as sequential map.

h2 { "Copy array storage" }

Unreleased language branch: copy(values) returns an independent array of numeric, Bool or String elements. It preserves length, order and exact numeric bits. String text remains immutable and may share its own backing. Copying records, function values or nested mutable data is not supported until their copy-depth rules are defined.

h2 { "Replace a numeric array binding" }

Unreleased language branch: a numeric array name can be reassigned in CPU functions, including functions with task suspension or expected-error handling. The new array may have a different length, but its numeric element type stays fixed. Reassignment updates that local name; previous aliases still refer to their original storage. This differs from sort(values), which writes the existing array.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> [int]:
  values = [3, 1, 2]
  original = values
  values = sorted(values)
  values = concat(values, [4])
  [original[0], length(values), values[0]]
""" }
The result is [3, 4, 1]. Branches and while loops carry the current array reference. Ordinary numeric-array parameters and captures may replace their own private name; that does not grant permission to modify their original read-only storage. A replacement with fresh storage provides writable data, while assigning another read-only array preserves its read-only access path.

Bool, String, record and function-array name reassignment is not included yet. Functions that combine replacement with parallelMap currently support numeric output arrays only; Bool and owned-element outputs remain gated. Source arrays also remain unsupported in mixed GPU supervisors. Element assignment and sort on existing numeric or Bool storage follow the separate rules below.

A task continues reading the storage it received even when the parent replaces its local name. Fresh replacement storage can be modified independently. An alias of the old array remains protected until its task readers settle.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn readFirst(values: [int]) -> int:
  values[0]
fn main() -> int ! TaskError:
  values = [3, 1, 2]
  oldFirst = try parallel:
    reader = spawn readFirst(values)
    values = sorted(values)
    values[0] = 9
    try await reader
  oldFirst * 100 + values[0]
""" }
The result is 309: the reader sees the original array, while the parent edits the fresh sorted array. The current access proof can conservatively reject this pattern when the same allocation site repeats inside a loop. Await the old readers before editing the replacement if it cannot prove that the instances are independent.

h2 { "Slice and concatenate" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> [int]:
  values: [int] = [1, 2, 3, 4]
  concat(slice(values, 0, 2), [9])
""" }

slice uses a checked half-open range. concat requires matching element types. Both allocate fresh outer array storage and preserve element order.

h2 { "Enter a numerical pipeline" }

Use tensor(array) for a rank-one numeric tensor, or tensor(array, dimensions) for a checked row-major shape. array(tensor) converts supported numeric tensor contents back into a flat array. Neither conversion changes the numeric element type.

In the unreleased language branch, both conversions copy numerical storage. Editing a source array cannot change an immutable tensor snapshot, and editing an array produced from a tensor cannot change that tensor.

h2 { "Edit local elements" }

Unreleased language branch: a fresh numeric or Bool array supports checked element assignment. A plain binding aliases the same backing; copy creates independent outer storage. A helper that writes caller-owned storage declares a mut parameter.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn update(mut values: [int]) -> int:
  values[1] = 9
  values[1]

fn main() -> int:
  values = [1, 2, 3]
  alias = values
  snapshot = copy(values)
  updated = update(values)
  updated * 100 + alias[1] * 10 + snapshot[1]
""" }

This returns 992: values and alias both see the edit, while snapshot still contains 2 at index 1. Element assignment is a statement. Supply a separate result expression at the end of a function, and preserve the array's element type.

The destination and index are evaluated and bounds-checked before the right-hand side. A failing right-hand side does not perform that assignment. Writes completed before a later fault or cancellation remain visible; in-place updates do not roll back.

h2 { "Wait for shared readers" }

Ordinary parameters and captured arrays are read-only. Parent-provided task inputs stay read-only until their readers settle, including reads through aliases and aggregate fields. Await every relevant reader before writing, or edit an independent copy instead.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn read(values: [int]) -> int:
  values[0] + values[1]

fn main() -> int ! TaskError:
  values = [1, 2]
  try parallel:
    pending = spawn read(values)
    observed = try await pending
    values[0] = observed + 1
    values[0]
""" }

Replacing String, record or function-array elements is not supported. Tensors retain value semantics. Source-array operations currently require native CPU programs; the mixed GPU supervisor does not yet support array-valued locals. Use tensors for GPU programs.

